---
description: >
  Brevity-sweep audit of `.aide/docs/.aide` — frontmatter close to caps but Strategy and examples reproduce verbatim References-section example bytes, blowing the example caps and crossing the forbidden-content line.
intent: >
  `.aide/docs/.aide` governs the canonical AIDE methodology docs and the
  References section addition. Frontmatter is mostly in caps. Body is
  bloated by Good/Bad examples that reproduce full References-section
  Markdown verbatim, the same content the methodology doc itself owns.
misalignment:
  - spec-bloat
---

## Issues

- [ ] `description` is 139 chars (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `intent` is 77 words (under cap, fine).

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 4 items (cap met). Items are paragraph length —
  trim each to ≤2 sentences. Item 0 specifies the exact section position
  ("after `## Bad examples`"), which is layout detail belonging in the
  template/methodology doc, not in outcomes.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract per-item ≤2 sentences + Forbidden content (exact section name, layout detail)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has 4 items (cap met) but each is paragraph length
  with examples baked into the prose. Trim to ≤2 sentences each.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract per-item ≤2 sentences
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Strategy` has 6 paragraph-length decisions (cap ~5, ≤80 words each).
  Most decisions encode template/methodology details: "References is a fifth
  body section, not a frontmatter field," "each entry is a path plus a
  one-line description," "only notes that informed a strategy decision belong
  in References," "the synthesis agent logs references as a side effect,"
  "descriptions are breadcrumbs, not summaries," "References paths are not
  required to be valid links." Most of these are content the methodology
  doc itself describes; the spec should commit to "the methodology gains a
  References section that audits which brain notes informed the strategy"
  and let `aide-spec.md` carry the format details.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Brevity Contract caps + Sibling-redundancy with the methodology doc itself
  Misalignment: spec-bloat, spec-gap
  Category: Brevity, Sibling-redundancy

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces three full References-section blocks
  verbatim with exact path strings, exact descriptions, exact prose. These
  are byte-faithful template content — the spec is doubling as a section
  template. Cap is 2-3 brief examples; forbidden content rule forbids exact
  strings. Compress to "show one short example demonstrating
  path-plus-description format; one example demonstrating note-supporting-
  multiple-decisions."
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (exact strings)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Bad examples` reproduces full padded-list, summary-restating, and
  machine-readable-metadata References blocks verbatim. Same forbidden-content
  pattern. Compress to brief prose descriptions of each anti-pattern.
  Traces to: `## Bad examples` | Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

## Retro

This spec was authored to add the References section to the AIDE methodology.
The bloat traces to a writer instinct to provide the canonical examples in
the spec to "anchor the contract." But the spec is meant to govern *whether*
the section is added and *what its purpose is*, not to provide the section's
template content — that's `aide-template.md`'s job. The fix is structural
prune: examples become brief illustrations, the methodology doc owns the
template, the spec owns the why.
